Refractions: 3-argument max() does not exist in any shading language.

`applyRefractions` reduces a COLOURED fresnel F0 to a scalar with

    midf refractF0 = max( pixelData.F0.x, pixelData.F0.y, pixelData.F0.z );

but GLSL, HLSL and Metal all define max() with two arguments only. The
three-argument form is Ogre's own `max3` macro (Hlms/Common/*/
CrossPlatformSettings_piece_all.*), which is exactly what is meant here.

The line is inside `@else` of `@property( !fresnel_scalar )`, i.e. it is
reached whenever fresnel is a colour rather than a scalar — which is every
metallic-workflow material we author. The result is that the first refractive
(HlmsPbsDatablock::Refractive) object in a scene fails to compile its pixel
shader; the exception escapes renderOneFrame and the whole frame is lost, not
just that object.

Reproduced on Vulkan/glslang, driver 595.84:
    ERROR: 0:1145: 'max' : no matching overloaded function found

Media-only: this is a shader template staged into bin/media at build time, so
applying it needs no Ogre rebuild — just the patch loop and a re-stage.

diff --git a/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Refractions_piece_ps.any b/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Refractions_piece_ps.any
--- a/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Refractions_piece_ps.any
+++ b/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Refractions_piece_ps.any
@@ -19,7 +19,7 @@
 	@property( !fresnel_scalar )
 		midf refractF0 = pixelData.F0;
 	@else
-		midf refractF0 = max( pixelData.F0.x, pixelData.F0.y, pixelData.F0.z );
+		midf refractF0 = max3( pixelData.F0.x, pixelData.F0.y, pixelData.F0.z );
 	@end

 	// refractNormal must be in view space, and we ignore .z component
